speechtext:
As this section should
he generally discussed, my right hon. friend (Sir Robert Borden) should explain to the committee the reasons for making such a radical departure from the provisions of the Militia Act in providing for ten different classes. In the Militia Act, there are only four classes. First there is the distinction between unmarried men and married men, and there are only two classes of unmarried men and two classes of married men, so that, apart from the question of marriage there are only two classes. Section 15 .of the Militia Act says:
The male population liable to serve in the militia shall be divided into four classes:
The first class shall comprise all those of the age of eighteen years and upwards, but under thirty years, who are unmarried or widowers without children;
The second class shall comprise all those of the age of thirty years and upwards, but under forty-five years, who are unmarried or widowers without children ;
The third class shall comprise all those of the age of eighteen years and upwards, but under forty-five years, who are married or widowers with children;
The fourth class shall comprise all those of the age of forty-five years and upwards, but under sixty years.
The Militia Act provides for only two classes of unmarried men, those from 18 to 30, and those from 30 to- 45 years of age. This Bi'll creates ten classes, some of them married and some unmarried. The first class is from 20 to 24; the second from 24 to 28, and the third from 28 to 34. It seems to me that the committee should be apprised of the reasons for this very grave departure
from the provisions of the Militia Act. I myself am unable to see why there should be a distinction between boys from 20 to 24 and young men from 24 to 28. There may be some very good reasons for it, but there would seem to be less justice in calling out first of all boys from 20 to 24 years of age, for they were mere boys when the war broke out and never had the opportunity of voting for members for this Parliament. But they are to be the first ones called out to serve the State, and just why they should be [DOT] called upon to bear the first burden of military service I am unable to understand.
